  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 113 PM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nBy Thursday the first of a few upper troughs moves out of the \nWestern US and begins to beat back on the ridge. This one, though, \npasses well to the north of our area, near the Canadian border. As a \nresult, we're not expecting any rain with it and the frontal \nboundary trailing it won't make much progress southward either. Thus \nwe see another few days of warming, with highs near 70 Thursday, \nFriday, and Saturday. By late this week, Gulf moisture finally \nbegins to return as well, with dewpoints climbing into the 50s, \nbuoying nighttime low temperatures even further.\n\nThe next significant trough moves out of the Rockies on Saturday and \ntracks east toward the Mississippi River on Sunday. Guidance varies \non how sharp this trough axis will be as it nears our area. Most \nguidance has the bulk of this wave passing by to our north with \nlittle in the way of convergence along the ensuing cold front to \nproduce much precipitation locally. However, some guidance sources, \nincluding the latest 12Z GFS produce a sharper, deeper trough with \nmore forcing for rainfall locally Saturday night into Sunday. The \ntrends among the ensemble guidance show a gradual slowing and \ndeepening of this wave over the last 48 hours of model runs, leading \nto greater rain chances locally. Among the 12Z LREF, about 90 \npercent of members produced at least 0.01 inch of precipitation in \nthe 24 hours ending midday Sunday. Timing uncertainty has kept NBM \nPOP a bit lower, in the 50 to 60 percent range, still favoring \nSaturday night. While this represents our next best chance for rain, \nit may not amount to much. Among the same 12Z LREF, up to 60 percent \nof members produce more than 0.10 inches of precipitation locally, \nand only up to 30 percent produce 0.25 inches.\n\nSunday's trough pushes a cold front eastward along with it, pushing \nout the mild and moist air mass and ushering in a cooler and drier \none. There's some uncertainty on how cool it gets behind this front, \nas guidance varies on how quickly ridging builds back in behind the \ntrough and how much cloud cover lingers. However, all guidance \nindicates a significant cool down of about 15 degrees. High \ntemperatures dip back into the 40s, similar to last weekend. Low \ntemperatures also dip back to around freezing, but this air mass is \nnot likely to be as dry as the last one, so overnight lows are \nlikely to be a few degrees warmer than last weekend. These \nseasonably cool temperatures last at least through the middle of \nnext week.\n\nThere's some indication of another trough around midweek that could \nprovide another chance of precipitation, but guidance is \nconsiderably more variable in the handling of this trough and \nwhether any rain falls locally. The greatest consensus right now \nfavors a southerly track near the Gulf Coast, but among the 00Z LREF \nup to 50 percent of members do produce measurable precipitation \nlocally.",
